WEBVTT

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.636
So in the last video we
looked at adding force

00:00:06.648 --> 00:00:10.560
and being able to tidy
up our script to make

00:00:10.561 --> 00:00:13.246
sure that we can return
the specific values of

00:00:13.247 --> 00:00:17.080
random torque, random
spawn, and random force.

00:00:17.081 --> 00:00:21.520
Now what we're going to do is test a mouse
press so we can detect a mouse click with

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:26.920
UNC's newer input system so we can directly
access something without actually defining

00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:29.887
an input action. So we
want to make sure that

00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:34.959
our target script is open. I've just added
the update method in between start and the

00:00:34.971 --> 00:00:37.480
helper methods just so
that we can start using

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.645
it in a better place.
So to do this is we can

00:00:41.657 --> 00:00:45.520
do if and then we'll
say mouse dot current

00:00:45.521 --> 00:00:52.380
dot left button was pressed this frame and
then make sure to end that add two curly

00:00:53.894 --> 00:01:01.263
braces and we can write debug dot log
in brackets and clause mouse was clicked.

00:01:01.264 --> 00:01:03.937
So in the update method
here you can see that

00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:06.520
we're using a more
shorter line to access the

00:01:06.521 --> 00:01:09.157
functionality of a mouse
click. So while we're

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:11.760
writing it like this is
great for prototyping

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.606
or testing things out
without setting up a

00:01:14.618 --> 00:01:17.680
full input actions map
or anything like that.

00:01:17.681 --> 00:01:20.060
So with the mouse you
can set that to anything

00:01:20.072 --> 00:01:22.360
else you can set that
to something like game

00:01:22.361 --> 00:01:26.070
pad as in there or you
can set it to something

00:01:26.082 --> 00:01:29.480
like a keyboard but
we'll keep it on mouse

00:01:29.481 --> 00:01:33.480
and then dot current is just the current
mouse that you'll be using. The left button

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.577
is a button which is
in the control map by

00:01:36.589 --> 00:01:39.920
default and was pressed
this frame just means

00:01:40.019 --> 00:01:45.400
that it will return true. If in this frame
it was pressed down and it's perfect for

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:51.085
a click detection and then we just have
a debug log inside that if you need it.

00:01:51.086 --> 00:01:54.087
Do be aware that if a
mouse doesn't exist or isn't

00:01:54.099 --> 00:01:56.480
being used and you
use mouse dot current

00:01:56.481 --> 00:01:59.135
it will throw an error.
So be sure to be aware

00:01:59.147 --> 00:02:01.640
of that and if you do
need multiple devices

00:02:01.641 --> 00:02:06.520
rebinding or any of the other things you
can use the input actions like we've looked

00:02:06.819 --> 00:02:12.040
at in other parts of this series. So now if
we test this out and I left click you can

00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:18.320
see that we do get the debug log at the top
which his mouse was clicked. So I want you

00:02:18.321 --> 00:02:21.488
to go into your target
script and go to your

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.680
update method and
make sure you write if and

00:02:24.681 --> 00:02:27.358
then in brackets a
mouse dot current dot left

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:30.886
button dot was pressed this
frame and then when inside the

00:02:30.898 --> 00:02:34.529
curly braces you can put
input dot log mouse was clicked.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.960
And so this is just a nice
way to make sure that you can

00:02:37.972 --> 00:02:46.240
check your input in a
nice simple line of code.